<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-preliminary-chapter" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>The Preliminaries</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> Statistics is all about making sense of our world from the information that we gather. 
      Our hope is that this book will be the starting point of that sense-making journey for you! 
    </p>
      
    <p>Statistics encompasses methods for data collection, analysis, and interpretation. 
      Statistical methods influence our lives daily. They help to determine the medicines that are available to us, 
      the ads that we get from the grocery store, and the websites our search engines refer us to. They are essential 
      to weather forecasts, insurance rates, and the quality control of the products we buy. We use them to keep track 
      of our health, the economy, and social issues such as race and gender equality, crime rates, and poverty. 
      We see them in the form of graphics and charts, means and percentages, conclusions and predictions in academic 
      research, media reports, and advertisements. <alert>cite and paraphrase</alert>
    </p>
     
    <p>This preliminary chapter will introduce you to the concepts that you will learn throughout this book. 
      We start by taking a look at the bigger picture so that when you learn the details in later chapters, you'll understand how 
      they fit into the larger whole. 
    </p>  
  </introduction>

